    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=592272295_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Dean of Students for Campus Life and Conduct",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nAbout the Role Albright College seeks a Dean of Students for Campus Life and Conduct to lead its campus life program and student conduct process. This hybrid role — common at small colleges — combines strategic oversight residence life and student engagement with administration of a fair, educationally grounded conduct system. The Dean serves as a key member of the Student Affairs leadership team, a trusted advisor to the Vice President for Student Affairs, and a central resource for students navigating both community life and conduct matters. Principal Responsibilities Direct residential life strategy, staffing, budget, and programming, including supervision of professional and student staff (Community Coordinators, Resident Assistants). Administer the student conduct process in its entirety — case management, hearings, sanctioning, and appeals — in compliance with FERPA, Title IX, and the Clery Act. Serve as primary hearing officer for routine cases and provide oversight of conduct boards when appropriate. Oversight of student engagement including student organizations, Student Government and Greek Life. Participate in the senior on-call rotation for crisis response and after-hours emergencies. Develop and revise institutional policy, including the Student Handbook, Code of Conduct, and Housing Agreement. Qualifications Master's degree in Higher Education Administration, Student Affairs, Counseling, or a related field. A minimum of five years of experience in residence life, student conduct, or student affairs, including at least two years of supervisory experience. Demonstrated knowledge of student development theory, due process standards, and relevant federal and state regulations. Experience in an on-call or crisis-response capacity, and with housing or conduct management software (e.g., The Housing Director, Maxient). Sound judgment, discretion, and strong written and verbal communication skills",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=591461728_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Men's Ice Hockey Team Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPosition Summary The Albright College Men's Hockey Team Equipment Manager & Social Media Manager will support the daily operations, organization, and public presentation of the Albright Men's Hockey program. This position combines responsibility for team equipment and locker room operations with management of the team's social media presence. The Equipment Manager will work closely with the coaching staff and players and will travel with the team for road games and competitions. The ideal candidate is organized, dependable, responsible, and comfortable working in a fast-paced athletic environment. An interest in hockey, athletics, sports media, photography, video, or communications is preferred. This position requires the ability and willingness to travel with the team for road games. The student employee should expect to work some evenings, early mornings, weekends, and extended hours on game days and travel days. All work hours, including approved team travel responsibilities, are subject to the Federal Work Study 19-hour-per-week maximum. Equipment Manager Responsibilities Maintain organization and cleanliness of the team's locker room and equipment areas. Assist coaches and staff with equipment needs before and after practices and games. Prepare the locker room and team areas for home games. Prepare equipment and team supplies for road games. Pack, organize, load, and unload team equipment for road trips. Travel with the team to road games and assist with equipment responsibilities throughout the trip. Ensure equipment and team supplies arrive at road venues and are properly organized. Assist with equipment setup and breakdown at home and away games. Assist with laundry and organization of team practice and game apparel as assigned. Identify equipment or supply needs and communicate them to the coaching staff. Perform other hockey operations duties as assigned by the coaching staff. Social Media Responsibilities Assist in managing Albright Men's Hockey social media accounts. Provide social media coverage during home and away games. Create and publish game-day content, including pregame information, in-game updates, highlights, and final results. Create graphics and promotional content using available design tools. Promote home games, special events, community activities, and team initiatives. Coordinate content with the Head Coach and Albright Athletics when appropriate. Ensure all social media content reflects the standards and values of Albright College and Albright Men's Hockey. Road Game Responsibilities The Equipment Manager & Social Media Manager will travel with the team for assigned road games and will be responsible for supporting both equipment and social media operations while traveling. The ability to travel with the team for road games is an essential function of this position. Qualifications Must be an Albright College student eligible for Federal Work Study. Must be available to travel with the hockey team for road games. Strong organizational and communication skills. Dependable and punctual. Able to work independently and as part of a team. Comfortable working around college athletes and coaching staff. Able to work evenings, weekends, early mornings, and extended game-day schedules. Interest in hockey, athletics, sports media, photography, or communications preferred. Experience with social media platforms preferred. Experience with Canva, Adobe or similar programs is a plus. Work Schedule Maximum: 19 hours per week Hours will vary based on the hockey schedule and may include: Weekday practices Evening practices Home games Away games and team travel Weekends The work schedule will be coordinated with the student's academic responsibilities. All work hours must remain within the applicable Federal Work Study weekly hour limit.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=563926059_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Financial Analyst",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPosition Summary:  Albright College Institute of Nonprofit and Civic Administration (INCA) is a financial and operations consultant providing outsourced financial services for governmental entities, including municipalities and nonprofits. We provide efficient and timely public-sector financial reporting and operational deliverables for our clients, focusing on state and federal reporting, grants management, supervision of accounts payable, accounts receivable, payroll, month-end close, cash and investment management, audit and budget preparation and reporting and application implementation.  This is a part-time temporary consulting role to provide fractional services to nonprofits and governmental entities under the supervision of Finance Manager of Institute of Nonprofit and Civic Administration. Onsite services in the role may be 1-3 days remote or onsite depending on the client’s needs and systems.  Essential Job Functions: Assisting clients with supporting financial operations for municipalities and nonprofits including:  Bank account reconciliations  Assisting with implementing accounting systems Entering Accounts Payable and Accounts Receivable transactions Creating General Ledger entries Entering budgeting forecasts provided  Developing financial reporting Audit reconciliations Ensuring compliance with funding requirements.  Collaborate with Finance Manager on prioritizing assignments  Collaborate onsite and remotely with client staff to ensure efficient business services. Qualifications /Prerequisites SKILLS - Knowledge / Abilities Comprehensive knowledge of Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP) and the practices of Government Accounting Standards Board (GASB) Comprehensive knowledge of budget development and reporting Knowledge of federal, state, and local laws, codes, regulations, and reporting requirements related to municipal accounting and finance administration Knowledge & proficiency of information systems and related applications (MS Office / Excel / Word) Ability to manage complex projects and adapt to changing circumstances Ability to continuously create new efficiencies Ability to prepare concise reports and make effective oral and written presentations Ability to develop effective relationships with management staff, employees, governmental agencies, and service providers doing business with the municipality Strong analytical and problem-solving skills Ability to demonstrate honesty and convey integrity to maintain public trust Possession of current and valid PA Driver’s License Employment Experience  Governmental accounting and/or auditing experience preferred Education  Bachelor’s degree in Finance or Accounting or related field preferred Working Conditions Work is performed at client offices and involves frequent interruptions. Work involves trouble shooting and is in a high paced environment. The work will be assigned by the Finance Manager.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=563923329_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Student Financial Analyst",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPosition Summary: Albright College Institute of Nonprofit and Civic Administration (INCA) is a financial and operations consultant providing outsourced financial services for governmental entities, including municipalities and nonprofits. We provide efficient and timely public-sector financial reporting and operational deliverables for our clients, focusing on state and federal reporting, grants management, supervision of accounts payable, accounts receivable, payroll, month-end close, cash and investment management, audit and budget preparation and reporting and application implementation.  This is a part-time temporary consulting role for junior and senior students enrolled in Albright College’s Accounting and Finance Majors and will involve the provision of fractional services to nonprofits and governmental entities under the supervision of Finance Manager of Institute of Nonprofit and Civic Administration. This role may provide between 1-3 days of remote work as assigned. There may be an opportunity to work on site depending on the client’s needs and systems.  Essential Job Functions: Assisting clients with supporting financial operations for municipalities and nonprofits including:  Bank account reconciliations  Assisting with implementing accounting systems Entering Accounts Payable and Accounts Receivable transactions Creating General Ledger entries Entering budgeting forecasts provided  Developing financial reporting Audit reconciliations Ensuring compliance with funding requirements.  Collaborate with Finance Manager on prioritizing assignments  Collaborate onsite and remotely with client staff to ensure efficient business services. Qualifications /Prerequisites SPECIFC SKILLS - Knowledge / Abilities – As a current student at Albright College, the candidate will be familiar with the following, and this position will provide them with an opportunity to learn how they are applied  Be familiar with Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP) and the Government Accounting Standards Board (GASB) as introduced in course work. The selected candidate will learn how to apply these reporting practices in government and nonprofit accounting.  Basic knowledge of budget development and reporting Knowledge of federal, state, and local laws, codes, regulations, and reporting requirements related to municipal accounting and finance administration Knowledge & proficiency of information systems and related applications (MS Office / Excel / Word) OTHER SKILLS AND ABILITIES Ability to manage complex projects and adapt to changing circumstances Ability to continuously create new efficiencies Ability to prepare concise reports and make effective oral and written presentations Ability to develop effective working relationships with management staff, employees, governmental agencies, and service providers doing business with the municipality or non-profit Strong analytical and problem-solving skills Ability to demonstrate honesty and convey integrity to maintain public trust Possession of current and valid PA Driver’s License Employment Experience  Governmental accounting or auditing experience preferred Education  Enrolled as a junior or senior in Albright College's Finance or Accounting program Working Conditions Work is performed remotely with the possibility of work at a client’s offices. The work may involve frequent interruptions, trouble shooting, and will be in high paced environment.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=550415721_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Lions Teach Student Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPosition Summary: This position will work closely with the 13th Street Educational Partnership and students and teachers in the Reading School District, namely Northeast Middle, 13 th & Union Elementary, and 12th & Marion Elementary Schools, to provide tutoring services and teacher assistance.   Essential Job Functions: Tutors/Assistants will work directly with students and teachers in the Reading School District. Tutors/Assistants will work at the school site, in-person and on campus for special events/field trips when Reading school students are visiting the college. Tutors will work with individual or small groups of students to bolster academic skills, lead enrichment activities, and serve as role models.  Assistants will work under the direction of classroom teachers to support classroom learning and daily activities.   Candidates must have good communication skills and a willingness to provide support and mentorship to school age school students.   Qualifications/Prerequisites:   SKILLS a. Must have excellent interpersonal, communication, and organizational skills b. Must be patient, creative, and responsible c. Ability to develop rapport with children and manage small groups of children in classroom and club settings EMPLOYMENT EXPERIENCE a. Experience working with children preferred   EDUCATION a. All necessary clearances for working with children required b. Education students preferred   OTHER Federal Work Study students preferred",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=549958309_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Domino Players Student Costume Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPosition Summary: We are looking for Costume Design Majors to work in the costume shop as Costume Technicians. This position involves patternmaking, draping and sewing costumes with special skills in corsetry and tailoring. Essential Job Functions: Organizing the costume storage and shop. There are times student workers will be asked to work backstage as wardrobe crew during shows and to assist with wardrobe duties which include repairs and laundry. Maintenance of the Costume Shop sewing supplies and sewing machines is included in the daily responsibilities of the worker. Work Environment: The work environment characteristics described here are representative of those encountered while performing the essential functions of this job. The individual is routinely exposed to indoor conditions, minimum outdoor weather and temperature extremes, and moderate noise level. Qualifications/Prerequisites: SKILLS Ability to do basic sewing Experience in patternmaking, draping and skills in corsetry is not necessary, but will be learned on the job. EMPLOYMENT EXPERIENCE N/A EDUCATION Costume Design Major",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=537533033_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Student Peer Tutor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-28\n\nPosition Summary: Peer Tutors are academic student leaders who play an important role in supporting the Albright community. They help motivate and encourage students in their learning while providing academic support tailored to individual course needs. As members of the Peer Tutoring staff, tutors work with students through our CircleIn AI tutoring system, one-on-one, and in small-group sessions to review course material and strengthen academic skills. Tutoring responsibilities may include, but are not limited to: Supporting students through the writing process, including brainstorming, outlining, drafting, revising, and editing Answering questions and providing clarification on course content Reviewing course material and providing constructive feedback Helping students develop effective study strategies and create study materials Providing practice and preparation opportunities to reinforce mastery of course content Helping students develop general academic and study skills Peer Tutors work approximately 5 hours per week, with schedules based on student and program needs. Additional responsibilities may include leading class visits, helping design and present academic workshops, tabling at admissions events, and assisting with special programs and events. This is a great opportunity for students who enjoy working with their peers and are organized, patient, approachable, and comfortable greeting others and presenting information. Peer Tutors should be enthusiastic about helping fellow students succeed and committed to creating a welcoming and supportive learning environment. Essential Job Functions Complete required training by established deadlines Commit to at least 5 hours per week of tutoring throughout the semester Check email daily and communicate with tutees as needed Complete accurate and timely tutoring session summaries Communicate with professors or supervisors about student progress when requested Participate in semester self-evaluations Follow Student Academic Support and College policies and uphold the College’s values and expectations Qualifications & Requirements Strong academic performance and demonstrated mastery of the subject(s) tutored Reliable, flexible, and able to manage scheduling and email communication effectively Strong written and verbal communication skills Empathetic, approachable, and able to support students experiencing academic challenges Ability to maintain student confidentiality For Writing Support Tutors: strong writing skills, including brainstorming, drafting, revision/editing, research, and citation Experience Peer Tutors are selected based on academic ability, leadership, character, communication skills, and subject expertise. Tutors should be able to effectively communicate course content and support their peers' learning. Education Earned a B+ or higher in the course(s) they wish to tutor Faculty recommendation from the instructor(s) of the course(s) they wish to tutor Applicants will be asked to contact their professor(s) to complete the Faculty Recommendation Form",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=537500865_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Sports Information Student Worker",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-28\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPosition Summary: This position will help create an atmosphere aligning with the Athletic Department mission to represent, promote and maintain data for athletes from all demographics. Albright is an NCAA Division III member institution as well as a member of the Middle Atlantic Conference (MAC), and the Commonwealth Conference. For this student worker position, the student worker will assist the sports information director & the assistant sports information director, with day-to-day operations of the program. The chosen candidate will also share in social media and webcasting responsibilities and other duties assigned by director.   Essential Job Functions: Assist director with day to day operations. Maintain Albright Athletics Social Media Sites (Twitter, Facebook, Instagram) Design Game programs Write press releases/game recaps Maintain team statistics Compile statistics using Statcrew/NCAA Genius software program Design graphics for social media use   Qualifications/Prerequisites: SKILLS a. Flexibility b. Some computer skills c. Willingness to learn EMPLOYMENT EXPERIENCE a. Some experience working similar position is an advantage. Persons with little or no of experience will be considered if willing to learn. EDUCATION a. Student in good standing at Albright College",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=536730668_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Biology Laboratory Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nBiology Laboratory Teaching Assistant (TA) Position Summary: Biology Lab TAs assist in a specific class and lab section. This position is available to current Albright biology-oriented majors who meet the course and grade requirements described in the Education component of the position description. TAs answer student questions about biological concepts, laboratory techniques, experimental design, data collection and statistical analysis using Excel, dissection, and other aspects of working in a laboratory. They have a good understanding of how to work safely in a laboratory, and model these behaviors to the students taking the lab. Although lab TAs work under the supervision of the laboratory instructor, they are expected to be able to operate independently, and provide direction and instruction to students on how to accomplish laboratory objectives. Lab TAs may also be involved in testing the development of new lab activities, cleaning and organizing the laboratory, assembling and testing laboratory equipment, and monitoring student compliance with safe laboratory practices. Essential Job Functions: Students must have shown enthusiasm and a passion for biology in their lab courses, and have been reliable in completing course requirements (safety, on-time arrival and regular class attendance, assignments turned in on time, working well as part of a team) in their biology classes. Biology Lab TAs assist in performing a variety of laboratory tasks, including but not limited to, using laboratory equipment (such as spectrophotometers, micropipettes, laboratory glassware, gel electrophoresis apparatuses, microscopes, dissecting tools, as well as other equipment for specialized laboratory tasks), performing laboratory techniques (such as microscopy, gel electrophoresis, pipetting, measuring, dissection, and identification of organisms), and answering student questions. Lab TAs should be comfortable working with a variety of living and dead organisms. Some lab activities will require working outside with live organisms in a variety of weather conditions. Occasional preparatory tasks for future lab activities may also be required, within the confines of the scheduled lab time of the TA. TAs also assist with lab practical exams. Qualifications/Prerequisites: 1. SKILLS The employee should have good spoken and written English communication skills, feel comfortable explaining biological concepts, and be proficient at public speaking with small groups. The employee should be proficient in laboratory skills, including microscopy, pipetting, gel electrophoresis, sterile technique, spectrophotometry, measurement, dissecting, organism identification, basic Excel functions including graphing and statistical analysis, and reading and following directions from written scientific communications. 2. EMPLOYMENT EXPERIENCE Although no actual previous paid employment is required for this position, a student can only TA in Bio 151 or 152 for a course they have completed. Students who have completed either Bio 151 or 152 can TA for Bio 101. TAs for upper level courses may have additional requirements as indicated by the instructor of that course. 3. EDUCATION Required: Must be a current student in good standing at Albright College. Must be a sophomore, junior, or senior as of the fall semester first day of classes for the semester of hire. Must be a current biology major, interdisciplinary biology major (Biochemistry, Psychobiology, Secondary Education with a biology focus), or biology co-major. Must have completed a minimum of two semesters of introductory biology courses (Bio 151, Bio 152, and/or Bio 203), and completed a minimum of one semester of an introductory lab course (Bio 151 or Bio 152). Grades in the above courses must be a minimum of a B+ in the combined course (lecture plus lab). Students with a course grade of a B can be considered if their lab grades are in the A range (A-, A, or A+). Grade requirements for this position may also be met by students who have completed the introductory courses, and later earned a grade in the A range in at least one upper division biology course with a lab. Preferred: Completed both Bio 151 and 152. Ideally grades in the combined course (lecture plus lab) in the A range (A-, A, or A+).",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=536729652_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Chemistry & Biochemistry Student Laboratory Support",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPosition Summary This is a student position providing support for the teaching laboratories for chemistry courses. The student will assist laboratory instructors with preparation, setup, implementation, and cleanup of laboratory activities; help students during scheduled laboratory periods; and maintain laboratory materials, equipment, and spaces. Duties may be performed both during and outside scheduled laboratory periods as needed to support the effective operation of the teaching laboratories. Availability during a laboratory period of general chemistry is preferred. Essential Job Functions Responsibilities include the following: Assist laboratory instructors in preparing, setting up, and implementing laboratory activities. Obtain chemicals and laboratory materials from storage and return them after use. Distribute and organize chemicals, equipment, and laboratory materials at the appropriate locations before and during laboratory periods and collect and return materials after use. Prepare solutions and other laboratory materials using appropriate techniques and procedures. Maintain and organize laboratory equipment and supplies and clean laboratory and preparation spaces. Test experiments and laboratory procedures to ensure that they function as intended before use with students. Read laboratory instructions and provide feedback to the instructor regarding clarity, procedures, and potential issues. Record and analyze data related to course experiments as assigned. Answer student questions and monitor student progress during scheduled laboratory periods. Clean laboratory benches, cabinets, equipment, and other laboratory areas as needed. Dispose of chemical and other laboratory waste in accordance with college policies and procedures. Perform other tasks related to the preparation, operation, and functioning of the teaching laboratories in the chemistry and biochemistry programs as assigned . The student in this position must comply with all safety mandates, including wearing pants, a laboratory coat, and safety glasses as required while working in laboratory and preparation spaces. The position is open to work study students; the successful student candidate must have completed a college-level chemistry course with laboratory. Qualifications/Prerequisites: SKILLS The worker will have had experience with safe laboratory practice and with analytical balances and glassware.  EMPLOYMENT EXPERIENCE  No previous employment experience is required. EDUCATION The worker will have completed at least one course in chemistry and will have completed the laboratory from the course for which the worker is applying for (at Albright or the equivalent at another institution).",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=523258999_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Sports Medicine Aide (SMA)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-19\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nJob Description: The Sports Medicine Aide will assist in practice / game preparation (filling of water coolers, ice chests, and gathering of other sports medicine equipment), field set-up, fluid monitoring, field break down, and clean-up of coolers, as well as basic training room maintenance including making ice blocks, tape straps, heel and lace pads, cleaning whirlpools & tables, and other duties as requested by the Sports Medicine Staff. Hours worked in the Sports Medicine Department may be used as observation hours for further educational endeavors (ie: PT, OT School, etc.). Administrative duties may include data entry activities (EMR), as well as basic training room activities such as restocking supplies, cleaning whirlpools, filling and cleaning ice tubs, etc. and other duties as requested by the Sports Medicine Staff. General responsibilities: Opening of the facility: The first student scheduled is responsible for seeing that all ‘Opening Athletic Room’ tasks are completed within the first 15 minutes of their arrival. Then check the dry erase board for any specific items for that day. Whirlpools/Cold Tubs: Drain, Fill and clean at appropriate times or as directed. Clean as directed. Make sure pumps are off when not in use. Cold 55-65. Hot 102-110. ALWAYS Drain & Clean tubs at the end of the day, or as directed. Ice Machine: Move ice mountain during your entire shift. Fill bags appropriately for injuries. Instruct athletes how to properly make bags and self-wrap, if able. Empty scoop tray water periodically. Filing: Always check ‘To Be Filed’ bin and update equipment issue cards and injury files. Restock Supplies: This includes the taping areas, jars on counters, and modality supplies. Check for supplies needed from the storage room. Make a list of supplies needed and give the list to AT staff. Do this before and after the high use times for taping (2:30-4:30pm). Keep these areas organized. Cleaning: Regularly sweep and dust the facility, wipe down tables after each patient, wipe counters, rehab equipment, etc. as needed. Sweep and vacuum as needed. Generally, keep the facility CLEAN!! Remember this is a MEDICAL FACILITY. Laundry: Bag laundry and take it to laundry room as needed. Fold laundry and roll wraps when laundry returns. Use in-room laundry equipment as directed only. Put all clean/folded laundry away in its proper location. Team Medical Kits: Restock team medical kits at the beginning of each season and as needed when dropped off by coach. Take medical kit back to the coaches office after it is restocked. Games: Be 10 to 15 minutes early for game assignments. Help prepare for games as instructed. Help with all duties. No one is above the minor details. Follow instructions to help procedures run efficiently. Make sure all sports medicine items are taken out to the events and that all items return post event. Clean and dry coolers after events and return them to their proper storage place. Taping and Treatment Tables: Tables and pillows should be cleaned on a regular basis. Pillows and other treatment articles are to be returned to their proper place after each use. Modalities: Clean all modalities and replace parts neatly. Modalities, such as electric stim. and ultrasound, must not be used by any student if properly instructed student may set patient up ONLY staff Ats may turn on equipment. Biohazard Materials Follow Department’s Blood Borne Pathogen policy at all times. Remember when dealing with biohazard materials to wear gloves and dispose in proper container. Rehabilitation: supervise and Instruct with cues athletes on the proper techniques of stretching and rehabilitation as well as correcting improper techniques. The student may provide cues as to the progression of assigned rehabilitation exercises. Rules: Enforce all room rules, regulations and procedures. Each sports medicine staff member is expected set a good example of all rules and professionalism on and off the field ensuring a caring workplace free from hostility, discrimination or ridicule. Game/Practice Preparation: Review your sport set-up sheet for both daily practice and games prior to your scheduled shift. Check with AT staff for any changes due to weather, etc. The student who is scheduled last each day should gather all the necessary supplies for the next days contests BEFORE to closing the facility for the evening. Room Closing: The sports medicine aide scheduled last is responsible to see that all tasks on the ‘Closing Athletic Room’ sheet are completed before they leave.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=523516774_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Auxiliary Services Student Worker",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-19\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPosition Summary: Albright College houses an internally run on-Campus bookstore, a full gallery, and a nationally recognized Theatre and Theatre program. These areas of the campus are integral in being first points of contact for the College and potential students as well as gathering places for visitors from all areas of the region. Auxiliary Services Student Assistants play an active and important public service role by representing the college to various internal and external individuals browsing or looking to purchase merchandise, tickets, and artwork. Assistants will be tasked with working between these areas to maintain sales and distribution of retail merchandise, tickets, and information based on the area they are working.   Assistants are trained and responsible for being informed about current and upcoming performances, gallery shows, and merchandise so that they may accurately answer questions from potential patrons and customers of the College. Additionally, Assistants are responsible for handling money, transactions, and preparing advanced tickets, retail merchandise, sale events, gallery receptions and learning the different POS (Point of Sale) software used daily transactions. Assistants may be asked for help with other duties pertaining to the Bookstore, Gallery, and Box Office.   On occasion, some sales events may be held offsite and on evening/weekend hours such as pop-up shops for athletic games.   Essential Job Functions: Actively greet visitors, providing exceptional customer service Assist customers in-person, by phone and online regarding questions about book orders/fulfillment and store merchandise. Process transactions in the POS software, handle cash and credit transactions, including student charges to accounts. Actively participate in training, reading and understanding verbal and documented instructions for operations of POS systems and policies related to the store, box office, and gallery experience. Answer questions from visitors as well as those who call the phones. Responsible for learning and providing accurate information about each performance, existing and new merchandise, and exhibitions Inform visitors of theatre rules related to food/beverage, bags, photography, etc. Conduct opening/closing tasks as noted. Assist with set-up/tear-down and running of events, including catering preparation and beverage service. While on duty, notifying appropriate staff of security or maintenance issues. Applying for open shifts that fit your schedule. Assist with pricing, stocking, and inventory counts as required. Assist with shipping, packing/unpacking, and general cleaning bookstore, gallery, and box office. Taking attendance Other duties assigned may include, installation/deinstallation tasks, social media, research and working on the gallery archives   Qualifications/Prerequisites:   1.SKILLS Must have excellent customer service skills, including the ability to proactively engage visitors. Demonstrated interest in Albright College, the performing arts, retail, and visual arts is preferred. Ability to handle money and sales transactions in a secure and confidential manner. 2.EMPLOYMENT EXPERIENCE Prior work experience not required, but experience in ticketing or operating a POS system preferred. 3.EDUCATION Must be a currently enrolled student at Albright College",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=523648471_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Student and Campus Life Community Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-19\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPosition Summary · Live-on, full-time professional role responsible for fostering an inclusive, engaging, and welcoming campus environment. · Builds strong relationships with students and promotes belonging within the residence halls and through campus-wide initiatives. · Supports student retention, personal development, and academic success by serving as a visible, accessible, and trusted presence. · Ideal for individuals passionate about student development, community-building, and supporting diverse learners in a small liberal arts setting. · Lives in a college-provided apartment and collaborates across campus to ensure every student feels at home at Albright. Key Responsibilities Community Development & Student Retention · Build strong, inclusive residential communities by living among students and offering direct support. · Identify and assist students at risk of disengagement or departure. · Collaborate with the Student Success Coach to coordinate early outreach and retention-focused interventions. · Develop community-building initiatives that strengthen belonging and foster connections with peers, faculty, and staff. · Engage campus and community partners to enhance student well-being and the overall residential experience. Residential Life · Supervise, train, and mentor a team of Resident Assistants (RAs). · Participate in the professional on-call rotation for crisis response and emergency support. · Assist with key housing operations, including move-in/move-out, room inspections, and facilities follow-up. · Uphold community standards to maintain a safe, respectful, and academically supportive living environment. · Maintain consistent visibility and approachability within the residence halls. Student Engagement & Programming · Plan and implement co-curricular events that promote involvement, leadership, and community—especially evenings and weekends. · Advise and support student clubs and organizations on budgeting, leadership development, and event planning. · Coordinate major student life events (e.g., Welcome Week, Homecoming, multicultural and mission-based celebrations). · Promote campus events through effective communication strategies and social media engagement. Administrative & Professional Development · Maintain accurate records for residence life, incident reporting, and student programming. · Attend departmental meetings, trainings, and campus initiatives supporting student success and retention. · Contribute to a collaborative, innovative Student and Campus Life team. · Perform additional duties as assigned. Qualifications Required · Bachelor’s degree. · Strong interpersonal, communication, and problem-solving skills. · Experience supporting students from diverse backgrounds. · Ability to work evenings and weekends as needed. · Commitment to student-centered service and inclusive community-building. · Must live on campus in a college-provided apartment. · At least one year of post graduate experience in residential life, student leadership, or peer mentorship. Preferred · Familiarity with event planning, student development theory, mental health and wellness, and residence hall operations. · Interest in supporting special student populations (e.g., international, commuter, first-generation students).",
      "department": ""
    },
