    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=483999885_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant, Center for Exellence in Local Gov",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-04\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPostion Summary: Position is responsible to and receives direction from the Director of the Center for Excellence in Local Government (CELG). The Administrative Assistant provides administrative, communications, and event support for CELG training programs, conferences, and membership activities. Responsibilities include coordinating registrations, maintaining financial and program records, preparing routine communications, assisting with event logistics, updating website and social media content, and supporting the day-to-day operations of the Center. The position is expected to use artificial intelligence (AI) and other productivity tools to efficiently prepare announcements, correspondence, meeting materials, and other routine communications while maintaining accuracy, professionalism, and consistency. The position also represents CELG at a variety of functions and events. Specific Functional Duties Training Programs / Events Coordinate the promotion of training programs and events by preparing and distributing announcements, email communications, website updates, social media posts, and other promotional materials as directed. Use AI tools to efficiently draft, edit, and format routine communications, promotional materials, agendas, event summaries, and other administrative documents while ensuring accuracy and consistency. Maintain accurate registration and invoicing of all CELG training and conference attendees. Maintain records of revenue and expenses itemized by program and event. Coordinate program and event locations. Develop and manage program and event timelines, checklists, and logistics, including signage, technology needs, facility setup, and catering. Administer registration at programs and events. Communications and Administrative Support Maintain correspondence, including mass emails of reminders, agendas, minutes, and special communications with municipalities, the County of Berks, CELG members, trainers, and sponsors. Assist the Director with preparing communications, promotional materials, presentations, agendas, reports, and meeting materials. Maintain and update mailing lists, contact databases, website content, and social media postings as directed. Financial Responsible for accounts receivable and accounts payable reporting. Deposit and record monies received, including program registrations and sponsorships, in accordance with procedures established by the Director. Process accounts payable check requests. Assemble and verify receipts and disbursements for monthly financial statements. Education High school diploma required. Post-secondary education or an associate's or bachelor's degree in Business or a related field preferred. Skills Proficiency with Microsoft Office (Word, Excel, Outlook, Teams). Familiarity with QuickBooks preferred. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Excellent organizational skills with attention to detail. Ability to manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced environment. Ability to effectively use AI-powered productivity tools to prepare professional communications and administrative documents efficiently. Excellent proofreading and editing skills. Experience Three years of progressively responsible administrative experience, preferably in higher education, government, or a nonprofit organization. Experience coordinating meetings, events, communications, and financial records preferred. Experience maintaining websites and social media platforms preferred.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=483997978_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Hazardous Waste & Environmental Safety Coordinator (Part-Time)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-27\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPosition Summary The Hazardous Waste and Environmental Safety Coordinator is responsible for overseeing hazardous waste management, regulatory compliance, environmental health and safety practices, and safety training across the College. This position serves as the primary resource for the safe handling, storage, and disposal of hazardous materials and works collaboratively with academic and operational departments to promote a safe learning and working environment. The coordinator will develop and maintain compliance with applicable federal, state, and local hazardous waste regulations; conduct inspections and assessments; provide training to faculty and staff; and support campus-wide safety initiatives. The position will interact regularly with departments including Facilities, Chemistry, Biology, Art, Fashion, and Theatre, as well as other areas that utilize hazardous materials or present environmental health and safety concerns. Key Responsibilities: Hazardous Waste Management Coordinate the collection, storage, labeling, and disposal of hazardous waste generated throughout the College. Ensure compliance with applicable environmental regulations, including EPA, OSHA, state environmental agencies, and local requirements. Maintain and manage chemical and hazardous waste inventories, Safety Data Sheets, records, and other required documentation Conduct routine inspections of hazardous waste accumulation areas and laboratories. Serve as liaison with licensed hazardous waste disposal vendors and regulatory agencies. Develop and maintain written procedures related to hazardous waste handling and emergency response. Safety and Regulatory Compliance Monitor campus operations for compliance with environmental health and safety regulations. Conduct safety audits and inspections of academic, laboratory, studio, theatre, maintenance, and storage spaces. Identify safety risks and recommend corrective actions. Assist departments with hazard assessments, chemical inventories, and safety planning. Support incident investigations and recommend measures to prevent recurrence. Participate in emergency preparedness planning and response activities. Training and Education Complete all required environmental health and safety training and maintain current certifications as required by regulations and institutional needs. Develop and deliver hazardous waste, chemical safety, laboratory safety, and related environmental health and safety training programs. Train faculty, staff, and designated student workers on applicable safety procedures and regulatory requirements. Maintain records of safety training and ensure required retraining occurs as needed. Serve as a campus resource on hazardous materials management and safety best practices. Campus-Wide Safety Initiatives Collaborate with Facilities, Chemistry, Biology, Art, Fashion, Theatre, and other departments to address environmental health and safety needs. Assist in the development, implementation, and review of safety policies and procedures. Participate in campus safety committees and related initiatives. Evaluate new programs, equipment, chemicals, and processes for potential safety and regulatory impacts. Promote a culture of safety through education, communication, and continuous improvement efforts. Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in environmental science, chemistry, biology, occupational safety, industrial hygiene, environmental health and safety, or a related field; or equivalent combination of education and experience. Knowledge of hazardous waste regulations, chemical safety practices, and environmental health and safety principles. Experience working with hazardous materials, laboratories, facilities operations, or environmental compliance programs. Demonstrated ability to develop and conduct training programs. Strong organizational, communication, and recordkeeping skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively with diverse campus constituents. Proficiency with Microsoft Office applications and database or recordkeeping systems. Occasional schedule flexibility may be required to provide training, conduct inspections, respond to incidents, or support regulatory activities. Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education, laboratory, research environments, or related areas. Professional training and willingness to pursue certification such as Hazardous Materials Manager (CHMM), Certified Safety Professional (CSP), Occupational Health and Safety Technologist (OHST), or related credentials. Experience with laboratory safety, chemical hygiene plans, and safety audits. Knowledge of OSHA, EPA, and state-specific environmental regulations. Physical Requirements Ability to inspect facilities throughout campus, including laboratories, workshops, studios, theatres, and utility areas. Ability to climb ladders during inspections Ability to distinguish colors on hazardous labels Ability to lift and move materials weighing up to 30 pounds. Ability to wear appropriate personal protective equipment and work safely around hazardous materials. Albright College offers a competitive compensation package commensurate with experience, along with comprehensive benefits including health, dental, and vision insurance; tuition remission; and generous paid time off. This is a full-time, in-person position based on Albright’s campus in Reading, Pennsylvania.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=362066994_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Financial Aid Counselor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-05-19\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPosition Overview Albright College seeks a dedicated Financial Aid Counselor to join our Office of Financial Aid team. This position plays a crucial role in helping students and families navigate the financial aid process, ensuring access to higher education through comprehensive financial assistance counseling and support.  This position is a key customer service representative for the Office of Financial Aid.   Key Responsibilities Aid Processing & Compliance Analyzes and processes financial aid applications to determine eligibility and awards, in accordance with federal, state, donor, and institutional guidelines. Requests additional information as needed based on analysis and review Performs verification to resolve discrepancies in awards and FAFSA. Notifies students of any changes in their aid eligibility. Utilizes information systems to input data, maintain databases, conduct research, and generates reports utilizing student data. Selects and notifies recipients for specific programs in a timely manner.   Student & Family Counseling Provide individualized financial counseling to students and families on aid eligibility, loan options, and repayment. Deliver group presentations and support admissions events. Administer and counsel students on private loans, VA benefits, Yellow Ribbon, and Tuition Assistance (TA) programs. Resolves financial aid issues and questions received via email, texts, and phone calls.   Scholarship & Special Program Administration Manage state grant and special program rosters through AES, including updates to enrollment, cost of attendance, and grant returns to ensure compliance with PHEAA regulations. Submit rosters and update student records to meet deadlines and compliance requirements. Manage reciprocating state grants Financial Literacy & Outreach Conduct one-on-one and group financial literacy sessions. Assist in the implementation of the university's financial wellness programming. Collaborate with campus partners (Academic Advising, Athletics, Student Accounts, Registrar) to enhance student support and compliance. Leadership & Training Assist in training of new staff and work-study students. Participate in professional development, trainings, and conferences. Other Duties Support campus-wide compliance efforts. Participate in some weekend events in support of prospective student enrollment and financial aid counseling. Perform other duties as assigned to support the mission and operations of the Office of Financial Aid and College. Qualifications: Bachelor's degree 1+ years of experience in higher education, a customer service role, or a related field. Strong interpersonal, communication, and problem-solving skills. Ability to manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced environment and maintain confidentiality. Intermediate to advanced experience in basic computer software skills, such as Microsoft Office products, specifically Word, Excel, PowerPoint and Outlook. Ability to interact with the Department of Education systems, including but not limited to COD, NSLDS, FSA Partners and EdConnect.   Preferred 1+ year(s) of experience in financial aid, student services or related field. Understanding of Title IV regulations. Understanding of state grant (PHEAA/AES) aid policies Experience with financial aid management systems, PowerFAIDs   What We Offer Competitive salary commensurate with experience Comprehensive benefits package including health, dental, vision, and life insurance Retirement plan Tuition remission for employee and eligible dependents Generous paid time off and holiday schedule Professional development opportunities and conference attendance support Leadership development programs Collaborative work environment with opportunities for advancement Reporting Structure This position reports directly to the Director of Financial Aid and collaborates closely with senior leadership including the Vice President for Enrollment Management.   Application Instructions Qualified candidates should submit a comprehensive application package including: Cover letter addressing qualifications Current resume highlighting relevant experience and accomplishments Contact information for three professional references (including at least one supervisor)   Please address your cover letter to the Director of Financial Aid and articulate your vision for supporting student success through innovative financial aid practices.   Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Preferred start date is flexible to accommodate the right candidate.   To Apply:  Submit materials through our online application portal.  For questions about this position, please contact Human Resources at  hr@albright.edu",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=c4e80d96-7749-44bb-aa20-2c7594475725&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=123813001_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Faculty of Psychology, non-tenure track",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-03-10\n\nLocation: Reading\n\nPosition Information Title:  Faculty of Psychology, non-tenure track Employment Type:  Full-time, on-site position, long-term renewable position Start Date:  August 2026 Application Review begins:  March 6, 2026 This is a full-time, in-person position , and applicants seeking only remote opportunities should not apply. Candidate Qualifications: Preferred candidates will hold a doctorate or a master’s degree in Psychology.  A background in development or cognitive psychology is especially desirable. Teaching experience is preferred. Additional Responsibilities: Academic advising of undergraduate students Mentoring independent study, undergraduate research projects, and internships Departmental service (e.g., supervising the Honor Society) Holding regular office hours Attending department and faculty meetings Required Application Materials to Submit: Interested candidates should submit: A cover letter addressing their fit for the position A detailed CV outlining relevant qualifications and experience A list of courses previously taught Contact information for three references",
      "department": ""
    }
  },
